hi there steve kaufman here today i want  to talk about  what i consider to be three of the  biggest myths about learning any  language  number one that you have to  practice speaking and focus on grammar  number two  that you have to go to school  and number three that it's difficult  and i'm gonna do this with reference to  some information that i got from steven  crash krashen i'm still  excited about about having had lunch  with stephen krashen in riverside  california  uh last week and at that time he gave me  a paper  where which is called can second  language requires acquirers rather reach  high levels of proficiency through  self-selected  reading  and in this paper he confirms  that the more we read  the better we learn and the higher our  score on  tests like toeic and there is research  to show  how many hours  of reading will give you what result on  toeic and i'm going to analyze this in  more detail in a blog post at my blog  in doing research for this  because this  uh paper is uh was produced by stephen  krashen and by beniko mason  who is an english teacher in japan  i also googled beniko mason and here  there's a very interesting paper called  self-selected pleasure reading and story  listening for foreign foreign language  classrooms  and both these papers stress the basic  fact that in order to  acquire a language  more than anything else you need to read  and it identifies how much you need to  read  and of course listening is also powerful  i happen to be a great fan of listening  because it helps prepare me to speak  because it's something i can do  while doing other tasks but i know that  i need a lot of reading in order to  acquire vocabulary and there's a number  of gems in both of these articles  about how  you know at the early stages most  learners of language never get past the  beginning beginner or early intermediate  stage so whether those people speak  absolutely correctly whether we hound  them on points of grammar that in any  case are going to take a lot of exposure  and practice  before it's going to click in maybe the  main thing is to get those people to  where they can communicate a little bit  without worrying about how correctly  they speak  and that's just one example there are  many more and this is so fundamental so  important  read and listen and therefore  you don't need to be instructed you  don't need to be corrected i mean once  you get to an intermediate level  the other goal of language teaching  should be to make you an autonomous  independent learner so once you reach  that intermediate level through lots of  reading and listening you will more and  more correct yourself or you'll seek out  some grammar explanations or whatever  wherever you feel that there are gaps or  mistakes that you keep making you'll  start to notice those if you are  an autonomous and motivated learner but  to get you to that stage rather than  overwhelming you with rules  and we can get people to choose things  of interest stories whatever they're  interested in to read and listen i know  i sound like a bit of a broken record  but it's so overwhelmingly true  and not everybody likes to read when  they don't know the words and and  basically that's what's behind link i  was the same way i had all kinds of  books in different languages there were  too many words that i didn't know i  didn't like looking them up in a  dictionary so therefore we developed  link as a sort of an assist but the  fundamental activity is reading and  listening and that will get you to where  you have a sufficient base in the  language so that you can then work on  the areas that you're weak in including  pronunciation but at least you have a  feel for the language you have some  vocabulary you're not discouraged so the  three again the three major mistakes  that people make they think they need  lots of instruction they got to worry  about grammar they got to worry about  output not true second of all that they  need to go to a classroom they don't it  can help if you don't have the  motivation but it's not necessarily  necessary and the third thing is that  it's complicated it's not complicated it  requires  time  and motivation  so there you go i kind of say the same  thing over and over again but it's so  important and so few people really grasp  it so for further information please  visit my blog i'll be putting a post up  there within the next week or so thanks  for listening bye for now
 

